Desilium packaging
==================

This folder holds the inert files a packager or a user installs, and the script that places them.
Nothing here runs at build time, and the session never reads these files except through the
standard locations after installation.

Layout
------

  wayland-sessions/desilium.desktop          session entry for login managers (Exec=desilium-session)
  xdg-desktop-portal/desilium-portals.conf   portal backend preferences for the Desilium desktop
  applications/org.desilium.Shell.desktop    names desilium-shell for KWin (X-KDE-Wayland-Interfaces);
                                             without it KWin refuses the shell its window list
  applications/desilium-settings.desktop     launcher entry for the settings application
  applications/org.desilium.Doria.desktop    launcher entry for the file manager
  icons/                                     hicolor icon placeholder; *.svg and *.png are installed
  install.sh                                 installs or removes everything (see below)
  arch/PKGBUILD                              Arch Linux package (builds the release binaries)
  debian/README.Debian                       notes for a Debian source package

install.sh
----------

  packaging/install.sh [--prefix DIR] [--target-dir DIR] [--dry-run] [--uninstall]

  --prefix DIR       default /usr/local (or $PREFIX)
  --target-dir DIR   where cargo wrote release/ (default <repo>/target, or $CARGO_TARGET_DIR)
  --dry-run          print every install/rm/rmdir command, write nothing
  --uninstall        remove the installed files; directories only when empty
  DESTDIR=...        staging root prepended to every destination (packagers)

Binaries come from <target>/release and are installed to $PREFIX/bin. The session file goes to
$PREFIX/share/wayland-sessions, portal preferences to $PREFIX/share/xdg-desktop-portal,
the settings entry to $PREFIX/share/applications, wallpapers to $PREFIX/share/desilium/wallpapers,
and icons to $PREFIX/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps. Missing binaries stop a real install before
anything is copied. Build first:

  cargo build --release --locked -p desilium-session -p desilium-kwin \
      -p desilium-shell -p desilium-settings -p desilium-notifications -p desilium-files

Desilium ships no compositor. KWin (kwin_wayland, KDE Plasma 6) is a runtime dependency of the
package, together with kglobalacceld (global shortcuts), kconfig (kwriteconfig6, used by
desilium-kwin), Xwayland, and xdg-desktop-portal-kde (screen casting, remote desktop and
screenshots). The shell must be installed in a directory on PATH, because KWin matches it through
the Exec line of org.desilium.Shell.desktop.

Dry run to review the file list without touching the system:

  DESTDIR=/tmp/desilium-stage packaging/install.sh --dry-run --prefix /usr

Installing into the running system is the user's decision; the script never runs by itself.
install.sh was not run for real, and the PKGBUILD was not built, since the move to KWin.

Arch Linux
----------

arch/PKGBUILD builds the binaries with --locked, runs the session crate's tests, and installs
through install.sh with DESTDIR=$pkgdir and --prefix /usr. The source line is a placeholder that
must point at a release archive; then run updpkgsums. Runtime dependencies are kwin, kglobalacceld,
kconfig, xorg-xwayland and xdg-desktop-portal-kde, plus the native libraries the -sys crates link
against (wayland, libxkbcommon, fontconfig, glib2, dbus) and the inter-font default face.
Kitty is the default terminal. Doria is the native file manager. GVfs backends provide network locations; the quick settings use WirePlumber, NetworkManager, BlueZ, brightnessctl and power-profiles-daemon when available.

Debian
------

debian/README.Debian lists the build and runtime packages and the install call for a debian/rules
file. It uses the same install.sh, so the file list is identical across distributions.

Why systemd user units are not used
-----------------------------------

Desilium supervises its own components (desilium-session) instead of shipping systemd user units.
The reasons:

  - The restart policy is part of the product: a KWin exit ends the session, shell, bridge and
    notification restarts use Desilium's backoff and crash-loop rules, and the readiness gate waits
    for KWin's Wayland socket. systemd can express parts of this, but not the socket
    hand-over, and it would make the session depend on one init system.
  - The session must work on distributions and init systems without systemd (the supervisor only
    needs a login manager and a session bus). Logind is still used for seat access, which is the
    one part that has to come from the system.
  - One process owns the whole plan, so `desilium-session --dry-run` shows exactly what will start.

Packagers who want systemd units for a local setup can wrap desilium-session in a user service; the
supervisor does not depend on that wrapper, and nothing here installs one.

Portal preferences
------------------

The session sets XDG_CURRENT_DESKTOP=Desilium:KDE. xdg-desktop-portal reads a file named after each
entry of that list, lowercased, so desilium-portals.conf applies first and kde-portals.conf is the
fallback. desilium-portals.conf selects the KDE backend for ScreenCast, RemoteDesktop, Screenshot,
GlobalShortcuts and InputCapture (KWin implements these, and only the KDE backend talks to them)
and the GTK backend for everything else. The file has a marked place for a native Desilium portal,
which is added once it exists. None of this has been run against a live KWin.
